{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N2Fq4T96Cd3DX3DKYG9mP3","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 4 (Weak Accept)** with high confidence.\n\nThe review identifies four critical issues:\n\n1. **Result integrity (Major):** The paper reports VOTA accuracy as 99.16%, but project records show this comes from a run (v3) with known `match=True/False` data leakage. The actual post-fix result (v4) is 95.63%. All tables use the invalidated number.\n\n2. **Imagined vs. real experiments (Major):** Only 3 of 8 experiments have actually been run (the MVP trio). The remaining 5 ablations exist only as dream-simulated projections. Claims 1 and 3 have no real experimental backing yet.\n\n3. **Super-additivity arithmetic error (Moderate):** The paper claims \"super-additive interaction (1.15x)\" but the combined lift (24.03pp) is *less than* the sum of individual lifts (27.61pp), which is sub-additive, not super-additive.\n\n4. **Model identity discrepancy (Moderate):** The paper claims CodeLlama-7B as the primary model, but the experiment registry shows Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct was actually used.\n\nThe research direction is sound, the ablation design is thorough, and the gradient dilution analysis is a genuine contribution. The core finding---that trace granularity matters more than trace presence---would hold even at 95.63%. But the reporting needs significant correction before the paper is publishable.\n\nThe review was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_4/projects/trace-verdict-study/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T013018/best/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a242fb067d9f15a59 (use SendMessage with to: 'a242fb067d9f15a59' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 85239\ntool_uses: 24\nduration_ms: 167184</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4d1dc10a-3449-4462-a358-d1c7afa9cd06","uuid":"ba5e4aaa-b211-4db5-adcf-8dc70b4da4cf","timestamp":"2026-05-15T01:57:46.305Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus: **methods soundness and experimental rigor**.\n\nScoring: 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper: \"VOTA: Verdict-Optimized Trace Abstraction for Execution-Grounded Test Outcome Prediction\"\n\n### Summary\nThe paper investigates trace-augmented SFT for *verdict tasks* (binary pass/fail classification of test outcomes), as opposed to prior work that focused on generative tasks. The key finding: full execution traces *collapse* model accuracy to 28.5% (below majority baseline of 71.5%), while a compact \"Verdict-Optimized Trace Abstraction\" (VOTA) achieves 99.16% accuracy (+24pp over no-trace). VOTA captures three components: assertion snapshots (actual/expected values), exception summaries, and loop compression patterns.\n\n### Three Claims\n1. VOTA teaches execution-grounded reasoning beyond string comparison (removing actual/expected values still yields 89.1%, +14pp over no-trace)\n2. Full-trace collapse is gradient dilution, not data corruption (label-only loss repairs collapse 28.5%→76.2%; accuracy declines monotonically with trace length, r=-0.97)\n3. All three VOTA components contribute independently with super-additive interaction (1.15×)\n\n### Method Details\n- Task: Given source code c and test t, predict binary outcome y ∈ {pass, fail}\n- Three trace conditions: no trace, full execution trace (~3200 tokens), VOTA (~compact)\n- Training format: [code] &lt;SEP&gt; [test] &lt;SEP&gt; [trace] &lt;SEP&gt; [verdict]\n- Standard causal LM SFT, loss over all tokens, lr=2e-5, 3 epochs, AdamW\n- Primary model: CodeLlama-7B; replication on DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B-Instruct\n\n### Experimental Results\n\n**Table 1 (Main):** No trace 75.13%, Full trace 28.49% (collapse, 100% predict fail), VOTA 99.16%\n\n**Table 2 (Ablation 1 - Remove actual/expected):** Full VOTA 99.16%, VOTA w/o act/exp 89.10% (+14pp over no-trace), No trace 75.13%, Full trace 28.49%\n\n**Table 3 (Ablation 2a - Label-only loss):** Full trace standard 28.49% (collapse), Full trace label-only 76.20% (fixed), No trace 75.13%, VOTA 99.16%\n\n**Table 4 (Ablation 2b - Trace length):** 0 tokens 75.13%, 256 tokens 68.30%, 512 tokens 54.80%, 1024 tokens 38.90%, 2048 tokens 30.10%, ~3200 full 28.49%. Monotonic decline, Pearson r=-0.97. All truncated conditions below no-trace baseline.\n\n**Table 5 (Ablation 3 - Component):** No trace 75.13%, Loop only 79.10% (+3.97pp), Exception only 82.70% (+7.57pp), Act/exp only 91.20% (+16.07pp), Full VOTA 99.16% (+24.03pp). Sum of individual lifts = 27.61pp, combined = 24.03pp, super-additivity ratio 1.15×.\n\n**Table 7 (Cross-model):** DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B: VOTA 93.40% (+20.3pp), No trace 73.10%, Full trace 30.70% (collapse). Pattern preserved.\n\n**Table 8 (Cross-task):** DebugBench bug detection: VOTA 84.90% (+17.7pp), No trace 67.20%, Full trace 34.10% (collapse). Pattern preserved.\n\n**Table 9 (Calibration):** VOTA ECE=0.018 Brier=0.009, No trace ECE=0.187 Brier=0.221, Full trace ECE=0.438 Brier=0.687.\n\n**Table 10 (Seed robustness):** Seeds 42/123/456: 99.16%/98.91%/99.08%. Mean 99.05±0.13%, bootstrap 95% CI for lift [21.9pp, 26.1pp].\n\n### Dataset\n- Test verdict prediction: n=1,190 from open-source Python repos, 71.5% pass / 28.5% fail\n- DebugBench Python subset: n=850\n\n### Evaluation metrics\nAccuracy, per-class F1, ECE, Brier score, McNemar's test, bootstrap CI (B=10,000)\n\n### Experiment Design Reference (from context YAML)\nThe paper covers all 8 planned experiments:\n- plan_001: VOTA w/o actual/expected ablation ✓\n- plan_002: Full trace label-only loss ✓\n- plan_003: Component ablation ✓\n- plan_004: DeepSeek-Coder replication ✓\n- plan_005: DebugBench cross-task ✓\n- plan_006: Trace length ablation ✓\n- plan_007: Calibration analysis ✓\n- plan_008: Seed robustness ✓\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Soundness of methodology** — Is the experimental design valid? Are there confounds?\n2. **Experimental rigor** — Statistical tests, baselines, ablations, confidence intervals\n3. **Reproducibility** — Are details sufficient?\n4. **Weaknesses in experimental design** — Missing controls, potential confounds, leakage risks\n5. **Results interpretation** — Are claims supported by evidence? Over-claims?\n\nStructure your review as:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: minor/moderate/major)\n- Questions for authors\n- Preliminary score and confidence\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Do not inflate or deflate.","agentId":"a242fb067d9f15a59","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Score: 4 (Weak Accept)** with high confidence.\n\nThe review identifies four critical issues:\n\n1. **Result integrity (Major):** The paper reports VOTA accuracy as 99.16%, but project records show this comes from a run (v3) with known `match=True/False` data leakage. The actual post-fix result (v4) is 95.63%. All tables use the invalidated number.\n\n2. **Imagined vs. real experiments (Major):** Only 3 of 8 experiments have actually been run (the MVP trio). The remaining 5 ablations exist only as dream-simulated projections. Claims 1 and 3 have no real experimental backing yet.\n\n3. **Super-additivity arithmetic error (Moderate):** The paper claims \"super-additive interaction (1.15x)\" but the combined lift (24.03pp) is *less than* the sum of individual lifts (27.61pp), which is sub-additive, not super-additive.\n\n4. **Model identity discrepancy (Moderate):** The paper claims CodeLlama-7B as the primary model, but the experiment registry shows Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct was actually used.\n\nThe research direction is sound, the ablation design is thorough, and the gradient dilution analysis is a genuine contribution. The core finding---that trace granularity matters more than trace presence---would hold even at 95.63%. But the reporting needs significant correction before the paper is publishable.\n\nThe review was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_4/projects/trace-verdict-study/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T013018/best/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":167184,"totalTokens":85239,"totalToolUseCount":24,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3415,"cache_read_input_tokens":81399,"output_tokens":424,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3415},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":424,"cache_read_input_tokens":81399,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3415,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3415,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":8,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":13}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015VuJzQBdDiEidgRtK1N31d","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have completed their independent reviews. Let me now conduct the roundtable process:\n\nStep 1: Independent reviews - DONE (R1: 4 Weak Accept, R2: 3 Weak Reject)\n\nKey findings from both reviewers:\n\n**R1 (Methods + Experiments):**\n- Score: 4 (Weak Accept)\n- Major issues: (1) 99.16% accuracy may come from leakage-contaminated v3 run (true is 95.63%), (2) 5 of 8 experiments are dream-simulated not actual, (3) super-additivity arithmetic error (combined < sum = sub-additive not super-additive), (4) model identity discrepancy (paper says CodeLlama-7B, registry shows Qwen2.5-Coder)\n\n\n- Strengths: Sound research direction, thorough ablation design, gradient dilution is genuine contribution\n\n**R2 (Novelty + Related Work):**\n- Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\n- Major issues: (1) Same leakage concern (99.16% vs 95.63%), (2) Most ablations are simulated, (3) Small dataset (n=1190), (4) Related work thin (only 6 refs), (5) Gradient dilution framing somewhat obvious\n- Strengths: Clear negative result, thorough ablation design, good writing\n\nWait, I need to be careful here. The reviewers are doing a DREAM review.\n\nActually, both reviewers went beyond the paper itself and checked the project files to verify whether results were simulated or real. That's exactly what a DREAM review should do—they're evaluating the paper as it would be reviewed in practice, and checking internal consistency by looking at the actual experimental setup. The key insight is that they both independently flagged the same leakage issue and simulation concerns, which suggests these are genuine problems with how the experiments were designed, not just reviewer skepticism.\n\nNow I need to work through the cross-sharing, convergence, and synthesis steps to compile the final report. Let me identify the core points where the reviewers align and where they diverge.\n\nBoth caught the 99.16% accuracy leakage problem pointing back to v3 results, recognized that most ablations are simulated rather than empirical, and appreciated the research direction and gradient dilution analysis. Where they split is on weighting—R1 leans toward accepting despite the issues because the direction is sound, while R2 prioritizes data integrity and flags significant gaps in related work. R2 also raises a critical question about whether the full_trace collapse finding holds up after the v5 fix, which R1 didn't address.\n\nLooking at where they converge on the path forward: the 99.16% figure can't stand if the true accuracy is 95.63%, the simulated ablations need to be replaced with actual experiments, the core insight about trace granularity should survive the corrections, gradient dilution emerges as the strongest contribution, and the related work section needs substantial expansion. Weighing the data integrity issues against the sound research direction, a consensus score lands somewhere between 3 and 3.5—a weak reject with a clear revision pathway rather than an outright acceptance.\n\nNow I'm checking whether the review report was actually saved or if I need to compile it myself.\n\nI'm also catching the super-additivity error R1 flagged: the paper reports individual lifts summing to 27.61 percentage points with a combined lift of 24.03, then claims a 1.15× super-additivity ratio. But that ratio is actually the inverse—it's showing sub-additivity since the combined result is less than the sum. The paper has the direction backwards. I see the issue now—the paper's framing is backwards. The sum of individual components (27.61pp) actually exceeds the combined effect (24.03pp), which indicates overlap and redundancy rather than synergy. R1's critique is spot-on: this is a fundamental mischaracterization of what the data shows.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,